No project file yet, so I'll suggest the two lightest options and show both.

---

### Option 1 — `TimeProvider` (built-in, .NET 8+)

.NET 8 ships `System.TimeProvider`. No extra packages, no custom interface:

```csharp
public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _clock;

    public InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? clock = null)
        => _clock = clock ?? TimeProvider.System;

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _clock.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

In tests, use `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing` (a small NuGet) or roll your own:

```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.False(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 6, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));

fake.SetUtcNow(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 7, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 6, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

### Option 2 — Tiny `IClock` interface (any .NET version)

```csharp
public interface IClock
{
    DateTimeOffset UtcNow { get; }
}

public class SystemClock : IClock
{
    public DateTimeOffset UtcNow => DateTimeOffset.UtcNow;
}
```

Same constructor-default pattern in `InvoiceCalculator`, and a test stub that returns whatever time you set.

---

**Recommendation:** If you're on .NET 8+, go with `TimeProvider` — it's already in the BCL and the optional `FakeTimeProvider` NuGet is tiny. The `clock = null` default parameter means callers that don't care about testing don't need to change at all.

